Othello README

OVERVIEW:
This program allows two players to compete on an 8×8 board, where each player can be
either human or computer-controlled with selectable difficulty levels. The game is
is structured around 12 main classes.
    The SetupGame class initializes the game, creates the board and referee, and
manages player setup and game resets. The Board class represents the game board and contains
OthelloSquare objects for each cell, handling piece placement, move validation, and flipping
captured pieces. Each OthelloSquare knows its position, whether it contains a piece, and
handles mouse clicks by notifying the Referee, while the OthelloPiece class represents
individual game pieces, storing their color, position, and JavaFX graphical representation.
Players implement the Player interface, with HumanPlayer responding to user clicks and
ComputerPlayer performing automated moves. The Referee class enforces the game rules,
manages turns, validates moves, updates scores, highlights valid squares for human players,
and determines the winner when the game ends. The GUI is handled by the PaneOrganizer class
and Controls (controlsPane), which provide menus for selecting player types, buttons for
applying settings, resetting the game, and quitting, while arranging the board and controls
within a JavaFX layout.

DESIGN CHOICES:
1. Using a 2D OthelloSquare[][] array containing Rectangles, unlike in Tetris where the array is
empty and can track occupied cells. Therefore, each square can be highlighted (change color),
and the piece can be added into it.

2. activate(Board board) and placePiece(int row, int col):
    The activate method is called by the current player. If the current player is human,
then nothing happens until the valid square is clicked; therefore, triggers the placePiece
method which calls switchTurn().
    If the current player is the computer, activate will get row and column from getBestMove()
and place the piece in that position. Similarly. in placePiece(...), switchTurn() is called.

